-- Ninth roster expansion: five Europe (Robin van Persie, Roberto Mancini,
-- Emmanuel Petit, Fernando Couto, Paulo Sousa) and five South America
-- (Diego Ribas, Lucas Moura, Daniel Bertoni, Cristian Rodríguez, Daniel
-- Fonseca), taking the playable roster from 110 to 120 (60/60). All ten
-- were promoted from the wrong-answer-only distractor bank to the playable
-- roster (research/data-policy.md's "Ninth expansion" note, CAREER_SOURCES.md,
-- DATA_AUDIT.md); two further researched wrong-answer-only profiles (Paulo
-- Futre, Paolo Montero) close the Portugal/Uruguay contemporary gaps this
-- promotion reopened.
--
-- Per the now-formalized derabona-player-addition skill (.agents/skills/
-- derabona-player-addition/SKILL.md, step 8), the frozen
-- 202609130002_ranked_roster.sql export is treated as permanently
-- historical and is NOT regenerated in place - this file is a new,
-- additive-only forward migration instead, the first roster batch to
-- follow that policy.
--
-- The hosted database was later found to have the real 80-player state from
-- 202609140003 rather than the rewritten 110-player baseline export. Keep this
-- still-unapplied migration self-contained by upserting the complete reviewed
-- 120-state candidates, players and memberships before rebuilding rivals. This
-- is harmless on fresh replay and closes every foreign-key prerequisite.
--
-- Generated by a reviewed one-off script (not checked in) that:
--   1. Loaded the CURRENT inline model (roster-data + game-model, 120
--      players) and computed its full candidates/players/memberships/rivals
--      via the exact same logic as scripts/export-ranked-roster.mjs
--      (game.candidates, game.eligibleRivals, game.matchTier,
--      game.similarity - not a second approximate matcher).
--   2. Parsed the *unmodified* 202609130002_ranked_roster.sql (still the
--      110-player state) as the prior applied baseline, since that file is
--      not touched by this migration and a fresh database replay applies
--      it unchanged before this one.
--   3. Diffed the two: new candidates/players/memberships insert as pure
--      additions. For rivals, all ten new players' own candidate rows are
--      new; a small number of already-playable players (Zidane, Maldini,
--      Neymar, Luis Suárez, Sneijder, Henry, Maradona, Figo, Baggio,
--      Francescoli, Vialli, Rui Costa, Valdano, Ramón Díaz) already
--      directly curate one of these ten names in their own
--      incorrectOptions (added by earlier batches' gap-closing passes,
--      back when these ten were still bank-only candidates with
--      "competitions":[]); those rows are stale rather than new, so this
--      migration deletes each stale (player_id,candidate_id) pair and
--      reinserts it with the now-correct tier/similarity/competitions.
--   4. Separately, and far more numerously: since promotion removes these
--      ten names from DISTRACTOR_PROFILES (the shared wrong-answer bank
--      every OTHER playable player draws generic fallback rivals from),
--      every one of the 110 pre-existing players loses generic,
--      uncurated access to all ten promoted names as rivals candidates -
--      exactly mirroring the guest/practice client's own
--      CareerGame.eligibleRivals() at runtime, which no longer offers them
--      either. This migration deletes those 1,084 now-invalid rows so the
--      ranked database's option pool stays identical to what the shipped
--      client actually offers; none of them were ever chosen as a live
--      round's dealt options (they are the matching aid's underlying
--      candidate pool, not a rounds/results foreign key), so no player-
--      facing result is affected.
--   5. Incidental discovery while independently verifying this migration's
--      own fresh-replay equivalence (unrelated to this batch's own
--      players): 202609150002_repair_stale_eighth_batch_rivals.sql
--      over-deleted two rows - zico|careca and zico|toninho-cerezo - that
--      Zico's own incorrectOptions genuinely curates (added in the eighth
--      expansion's own gap-closing pass) and that the current model still
--      produces with updated tier/similarity/competitions values; that
--      repair should have re-inserted the corrected pair, like its other
--      genuinely-stale rows, not deleted it outright. This migration
--      restores those two corrected rows too (on conflict do nothing, so
--      harmless against a database where some other patch already
--      restored them) - a one-line, independently-verified additive
--      correction, not a rewrite of that migration's already-applied
--      content.
--
-- Verified two ways before writing this file:
--   a. Fresh-database replay: parsing 202609130002_ranked_roster.sql alone
--      as "prior state", applying this migration's deletes then inserts in
--      Node (not psql), and asserting the resulting candidates/players/
--      memberships/rivals sets are byte-for-byte deep-equal, key by key,
--      to the freshly computed model - zero missing rows, zero extra rows,
--      zero value mismatches.
--   b. Upgrade-from-actual-prior-state: separately replaying the full
--      existing chain (202609130002 -> 202609140003_roster_sync_60_to_80
--      -> 202609140005_repair_stale_promoted_bank_rivals ->
--      202609150001_repair_stale_cannavaro_kluivert_rivals ->
--      202609150002_repair_stale_eighth_batch_rivals) with correct
--      composite-primary-key "on conflict do nothing" semantics confirmed
--      it reduces to the same 202609130002-alone state used in (a), except
--      for the two zico rows in point 5 above (independently confirmed and
--      restored).
--
-- No ranked_private.results, rounds, receipts or rate-limit rows are
-- touched; only candidates/players/memberships/rivals are additive here.
-- Never applied to the live hosted database as part of this change - see
-- docs/leaderboards.md's "Applying migrations to the hosted database" for
-- that separate, explicitly manual step.
begin;
